    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KPSR 221736\nAFDPSR\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Phoenix AZ\n1036 AM MST Sat Aug 22 2026\n\n.UPDATE...Updated Aviation Discussion.\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n- High temperatures reach or exceed 110 degrees nearly every day \n  through the next week and will be within a few degrees of daily \n  records, while morning lows remain unusually warm. \n\n- Extreme Heat Warnings are in effect through Friday evening with\n  daily major to extreme HeatRisk.\n\n- Thunderstorms will primarily favor higher terrain areas through\n  early next week, with drier and less favorable conditions next \n  week.\n\n&&\n\n.SHORT TERM /TODAY THROUGH MONDAY/... \nExtreme heat will be the primary weather hazard through the short\nand long term forecast. Early morning objective analysis shows a \n596dm high pressure centered over CO/NM. Models show that this \nhigh will slowly progress eastward through the beginning of next \nweek. Despite the center of the high moving away from our region, \nH5 heights over our region will remain in a 594-597dm range. With \nheights of this magnitude, the NBM continues to show afternoon \nhighs (morning lows) of 110-116 (83-91) degrees across the lower \ndeserts and 100-110 (70-80) degrees across the higher terrain \nareas. Temperatures of this magnitude are 5-8 degrees above daily \nnormals and some locations could tie or break their daily records \n(see Climate section below). These temperatures will lead to \nwidespread high-end moderate to major HeatRisk across much of the \nCWA and therefore the Extreme Heat Warning remains in effect.\n\nThe best thunderstorm chances will exist across the higher \nterrain areas through the beginning of next week with only slight \nchances for some isolated activity across the lower deserts. \nThunderstorm chances across the lower deserts for this afternoon \nare around 10- 20%. While some hi-res models (HRRR and RRFS) are \ntrying to pick up on some more scattered activity across south-\ncentral AZ (including the Phoenix Metro), they are likely \noverdoing the coverage. Models show limited moisture today (PWATs \nof 1.0-1.3\"), mixing ratios only around 6-7 g/kg, MUCAPE values \nless then 500 J/kg, and around 25-50 J/kg of CIN. The hi-res \nmodels showing scattered to widespread activity for this evening \nare likely picking up on some upper-level divergence and colliding\noutflows. While there will likely be multiple outflows moving off\nthe higher terrain this evening not much activity (other than \nsome isolated showers and maybe a few isolated storms) is expected\neven if they do collide due to all the limiting factors mentioned\nearlier. Strong to locally severe wind gusts and areas of blowing\ndust will be the primary hazard with any storm this afternoon and\nevening, with high DCAPE values (1800-2000 J/kg). \n\nModels are in decent agreement that there will be a slight \nincrease in moisture on Sunday and Monday (PWATs increase to \naround 1.1-1.6\" across the area) with the highest moisture \nexpected on Monday. Instability will be about the same on Sunday \nas today, but will increase to around 1000-1500 J/kg on Monday. \nAdditionally, mixing rations will increase to around 10 g/kg on \nMonday. Despite the increase in moisture, instability, and mixing \nratios models show an increase in CIN (100-200 J/kg). Activity \neach day will depend on how the day prior plays out. But right \nnow, Monday looks to have slightly better chances than Sunday. \nEither way, most of the lower deserts are expected to remain dry \nwith the best chances (and coverage) of thunderstorms expected \nacross the higher terrain areas to the east of the Phoenix Metro. \n\n&&\n\n.LONG TERM /TUESDAY THROUGH NEXT FRIDAY/... \nEnsemble members are in good agreement that by Tuesday drier air \nwill start to filter back into the region as the subtropical high \nstarts to retrograde back over our region. By the middle of the \nweek the center of the high will be straddling the AZ/NM border \nand by the end of the week the center of the high will be over AZ.\nAs the high moves overhead, PWATs will drop into a 0.8-1.2\" range\nacross our region and steering flow will be out of the S/SW. The \ncombination of these factors will lead to poor chances for \nthunderstorm development across the region. The NBM shows that \nTuesday and Wednesday there could be enough moisture around to \nsupport isolated thunderstorm chances for the higher terrain \nareas. But by the end of next week thunderstorm chances for our \narea shut off and will mainly be relegated to the Mogollon Rim. \n\nUnusually hot temperatures will be the main story next week. As \nmentioned above the subtropical high will slowly retrograde back \nover our region beginning on Tuesday, which will result in a \ngradual warming trend for our area. The high is also expected to \nstrengthen to around 596-598dam during this time. The combination \nof the center settling over our region and strengthening will \nresult in temperatures heating up to around 10 degrees above \nnormal for this time of year. Afternoon highs (morning lows) are \nforecasted to be in the 110-116 (85-95) degree range across the \nlower deserts and in the 100-110 (75-85) degree range across the \nhigher terrain areas. The increase in temperatures will also \nresult in an increase in HeatRisk, with widespread extreme \nHeatRisk forecast for the majority of the CWA by Wednesday. There \nremains high confidence in the heat forecast for next week. So, \nthe Extreme Heat Warning remains in effect through next Friday and\nwill likely need to be expanded to more areas by the middle of \nthe week and may eventually need to be extended into next weekend.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...Updated at 1735Z.\n\nSouth Central Arizona including KPHX, KIWA, KSDL, and KDVT: \nUncertainty in TSRA/SHRA coverage and corresponding gusty outflow\nwinds/wind shifts will be the primary forecast challenge through\nSunday afternoon under periods of mid/high cloud decks. Confidence\nis good that light E/SE or VRB winds will slowly transition to\nW/SW by mid to late afternoon. TS development over higher terrain\nareas may send gusty outflow winds towards the Phoenix terminals\nduring the late afternoon/early evening. Current Hi-Res guidance\nshows the best coverage of showers and storms will be over \nSouthwestern Pinal County, which would make a southerly outflow \nthe most likely/predominant favored direction. While direct TS \nimpacts are not likely at the terminals, briefly gusty winds and \ndecaying SHRA could affect operations early-mid evening. If more \nconcentrated storms can materialize along with multiple colliding \noutflow boundaries, better chances for isolated TS development in \nlower elevations will be enhanced with stronger and more prolonged\nwind gusts. \n\nSoutheast California/Southwest Arizona including KIPL and KBLH:\nNo major weather issues will exist through Sunday morning under\nmostly clear skies. Trends in winds should be similar to the past\n24 hours, generally varying between SE and SW with extended\nperiods of nearly calm conditions in between. TS activity over the\nBaja spine late this afternoon may send an outflow and decaying\nSHRA towards KIPL prior to sunset. Chances for direct TSRA impacts\nare too low to include in the TAF, however gusty SW winds justify\na continued mention through the early evening. \n\n&&\n\n.FIRE WEATHER...\nHot and seasonally dry conditions are expected nearly every day, \nthrough next week. Aside from a brief increase in moisture Sunday-\nMonday, minimum RH values each afternoon will be around 12-18% (15-\n25% Sun-Mon). Temperatures will be around 5-10 degrees above normal \nthrough the next week, with lower desert highs eclipsing 110 degrees \nmost afternoons. There will be slight chances for storms each \nafternoon through Monday, but they will mostly favor the higher \nterrain areas. Drier conditions, with lower storm chances are \nexpected by the middle of next week. Outside of thunderstorm outflow \nwinds, winds will follow the typical diurnal tendencies with some \nmodest afternoon upslope gustiness.\n\n&&\n\n.CLIMATE...\nRecord highs:\n\nDate       Phoenix         Yuma         El Centro\n----       -------         ----         ---------\nAug 22   113 in 2011    115 in 1969    117 in 1969\nAug 23   114 in 2011    115 in 2011    116 in 1969\nAug 24   115 in 2020    119 in 1985    117 in 1985\nAug 25   113 in 2011    116 in 1962    117 in 1962\nAug 26   117 in 2011    115 in 2020    115 in 1981\nAug 27   114 in 2023    120 in 1981    117 in 1981\nAug 28   117 in 2023    118 in 2023    117 in 2009\n\n&&\n\n.PSR WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nAZ...Extreme Heat Warning until 8 PM MST Friday for AZZ530>555-559-\n     560.\n\nCA...Extreme Heat Warning until 8 PM PDT Friday for CAZ562-565>567-\n     569-570.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nSHORT TERM...Berislavich\nLONG TERM...Berislavich \nAVIATION...Whittock/18\nFIRE WEATHER...Berislavich/Benedict \nCLIMATE...18\n",
